+# cluster ci-2d4c2b9f536c76c4 - macos-arm64 Qt 5 test-source compilation
+
+Pinned commit: `fa4a5f2e785c73e32216931e33cdbe140c06647d` (`050/m0-train-35`). Verdict:
+REAL, a test-source portability defect; not a product defect and not an infrastructure
+defect. The checkout was clean at that commit before any change. The pre-fix
+`tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp` hash is
+`6cf2870fee6131674b75d417240523c8a810cca41cf7caf1cec92da59a0b306a`, matching the task
+packet's `source_sha256`.
+
+## Evidence
+
+- `tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278` (before the fix) constructed the expected
+  list with `QStringList(channels, QStringLiteral("volume"))`. Qt 5's `QStringList` has no
+  `(count, value)` constructor; Qt 6's `QList` base has one. The hosted `macos-arm64` job
+  builds against Homebrew `qt@5`, so the test target stopped at exactly the diagnostic in the
+  finding: `no matching constructor for initialization of 'QStringList'`.
+- The expression was the only `QStringList(count, value)` construction under `tests/src` or
+  `src`; the other `QStringList(...)` uses in that area are single-argument initializer lists.
+- The failure is a compile failure in the test source, so it happens before any test runs; no
+  product code or runtime behaviour is involved.
+
+## Fix
+
+`tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp` now builds the expected list with
+`QVector<QString>(channels, QStringLiteral("volume"))` (the fill constructor exists on Qt 5
+and Qt 6) and converts it with `toList()`:
+
+```cpp
+// QStringList's (count, value) constructor is Qt 6 only; QVector's fill constructor is on Qt 5 too.
+QVERIFY(faders == QStringList(QVector<QString>(channels, QStringLiteral("volume")).toList()));
+```
+
+`#include <QVector>` was added. The expected content (`channels` copies of `"volume"`), the
+equality comparison and every preceding assertion are unchanged. No product behaviour, save
+format, undo step, command or audio-thread path changes.
+
+## Regression
+
+Added `tests/mixer-sends-on-faders-portability-check.py`, registered as CTest
+`MixerSendsOnFadersPortability` on Linux (the same pattern as the existing
+`Train51Portability` compiler regression) and listed in `tests/fork-sources.txt`.
+
+The checker reads the current `QVERIFY(faders == ...)` line from the test source and compiles
+it in a small fixture with the installed Qt 5 and Qt 6 headers (`Qt5Test`/`Qt5Core`,
+`Qt6Test`/`Qt6Core`), after checking that the line still depends on `channels` and still names
+`QStringLiteral("volume")`. It also compiles the original Qt 5 spelling as a negative control
+that must fail. The test is deterministic and compile-only: no timestamps, sleeps, races or
+wall-clock behaviour.
+
